# Editorial launch library. Each article has its own buying, making or brand question.
ARTICLES=[
('the-next-little-buzz','The next little buzz: why BeeGoods exists','Our story','bees','BeeGoods is the goods-focused next chapter beside Med Bee Tate. Here is what we are building, and what we want to get right.',[
('A small idea with room to grow','A quiet place can leave you wanting to take a little of its feeling home. That is the starting point for BeeGoods: bee-inspired goods with a clear story, useful details and an identity of their own. We are beginning with honey, wax, gifts and everyday objects as collection directions. Those are areas to explore, not a promise that every item pictured is already on a shelf.'),
('Let the details do the talking','Before a product becomes part of a collection, the practical questions matter. Who made it? What is it made from? What size is it? What does it cost, and when can it be supplied? Beautiful imagery helps introduce a mood, but it cannot answer those questions on its own. We want a product page to give you enough information to decide whether something fits your home, your table or the person receiving it.'),
('Follow the next chapter','BeeGoods sits beside Med Bee Tate within the wider Industry Army Marketing portfolio. The brands share a bee-inspired thread, while doing different jobs. Med Bee Tate develops the place and experience; BeeGoods develops the goods direction. Explore the collection pages, meet the connected brands and send a launch enquiry if you would like to hear more. There is no checkout or automatic mailing-list enrolment at this stage.')]),
('honey-label-questions','A honey jar should tell you its story','Honey','honey','A useful buying checklist starts with origin, the product description and the maker—not a beautiful label alone.',[
('Start with the actual description','Turn a jar around before deciding what its front label means. Look for the product name, quantity, origin information and the business responsible for it. Treat a landscape photograph or a Canadian-looking design as branding rather than proof of where the honey came from. If a detail matters to you, look for the words that actually describe it.'),
('Ask one useful follow-up','When buying directly from a maker, ask a specific question: where was this batch produced, or what does this particular description mean? A focused question is easier to answer than asking whether something is simply “the best.” Keep the answer attached to the jar you are considering; different products from the same business may have different sources or preparation methods.'),
('What we want our collection to explain','BeeGoods is developing its collection. Our intended product pages should make origin, net quantity, maker details and availability easy to find when those details are confirmed. We will not use a concept picture as evidence of a source or a certification. For Canadian labelling guidance, read the Canadian Food Inspection Agency’s honey labelling resource linked below; this article is a shopper’s checklist, not a compliance assessment.')]),
('honey-tasting-notes','Make your own honey tasting notes','At the table','honey','A simple way to remember what you enjoy without turning breakfast into a competition.',[
('Keep the comparison simple','Choose a small number of honeys you already have and label them clearly. Use the same kind of spoon and the same plain accompaniment for each. The point is to notice your own preferences, not to award an official score. If you change the bread, tea or serving temperature every time, you are comparing more than the honey.'),
('Write what you actually notice','Use everyday words. You might describe the first impression, the texture and what remains after a taste. “Good with this bread” can be a more useful note than an impressive word you would never use again. Keep the jar name and the maker beside each note so you can find that particular product later. Your notes are personal observations, not laboratory results or proof of origin.'),
('Build a table you want to return to','A tasting can be an easy gathering around a kitchen table. Keep portions small, let people choose whether to participate and retain the original packaging so ingredients and product information stay available. BeeGoods is exploring honey as part of its developing collection. When products are confirmed, the aim is to describe each one clearly enough that your next choice can be based on useful information as well as curiosity.')]),
('honey-gift-questions','Before you give honey as a gift','Gifting','honey','Think about the recipient, the occasion and the information that should travel with the jar.',[
('Begin with the person','A gift works best when it fits the person receiving it. Before choosing food, ask whether it is something they enjoy and whether they have any preferences or restrictions you need to respect. Do not replace that conversation with a wellness promise. A thoughtful jar can be a food gift without being presented as a remedy or a recommendation.'),
('Keep the useful information','Leave the maker’s label and packaging information attached. If you add a gift tag, avoid covering the product description or any instructions. For a hamper, keep a simple list of what is inside and which business made each item. That helps the recipient decide what to use, share or pass along without having to reconstruct the contents from a styled photograph.'),
('Let the occasion shape the presentation','A small thank-you may only need a jar and a handwritten note. A larger occasion might call for a carefully selected combination rather than more items. BeeGoods gift sets are a collection direction, not a currently confirmed offer. If you are planning ahead, tell us the occasion, approximate quantity and timing so the conversation can start with your actual needs.')]),
('beeswax-material-questions','Know what is in a wax product','Materials','craft','A material name is the beginning of a product description, not the whole explanation.',[
('Ask about the complete item','When you see a wax-based product, look beyond its colour. Ask what the item is made from, whether it is a blend and what other components are included. The container, finish, fragrance or packaging may matter to your choice just as much as the main material. An attractive photograph cannot establish composition.'),
('Separate the material from the claim','A maker can explain why a material suits a particular design without promising a health or environmental outcome. Treat broad words such as “clean” or “natural” as a reason to ask for specifics. A useful description should tell you what the business means in that product’s context. If a claim depends on a certification, look for the actual certification rather than assuming it from the styling.'),
('Our collection brief','BeeGoods is exploring wax-based objects and candles. Before an item is offered, we want the material description, size, maker and relevant care information to be clear. The craft footage and concept artwork on this site illustrate a direction; they do not verify the ingredients of a future product. Makers who can provide precise, plain-language product information are welcome to introduce their work.')]),
('candle-as-an-object','Choose a candle for the room it will live in','Home goods','craft','Shape, scale, scent preferences and product instructions all belong in the decision.',[
('Look at scale first','A product can feel very different on a large dining table and a narrow shelf. Check the stated dimensions rather than judging size from a close-up photograph. Consider the holder or container as part of the object, and make sure you understand what is included. A styled setting may contain accessories that are not sold with the candle.'),
('Respect personal preferences','Some people enjoy fragrance; others prefer none. Ask rather than assuming that a scent is relaxing or suitable for everyone. For a gift, a clear material and fragrance description is more helpful than a vague mood promise. If instructions or suitability details are missing, request them from the maker before choosing the item.'),
('Care belongs with the product','Use any candle according to its maker’s instructions and keep those instructions with it. This article is about selecting an object, not a substitute for product-specific use guidance. BeeGoods is still developing its home-goods direction. We want future pages to show dimensions, contents and care details alongside the visual story so you can make a considered choice.')]),
('soap-maker-introduction','What we want to know about a handmade soap','Maker stories','craft','Useful product information helps a maker’s work speak for itself.',[
('Tell us about the exact product','A soap introduction should name the product, its intended use, the maker and the full product information the maker supplies. “Handmade” explains part of the process; it does not tell a visitor everything in the bar or whether it suits their preferences. Send details for the actual item you would like considered rather than a broad description of everything your studio might make.'),
('Make the visual honest','Photograph the product clearly and distinguish finished goods from work in progress. Include a view that makes the packaging and size understandable. If a photograph shows several scents, colours or sizes, explain which variants it represents. Avoid adding performance or treatment claims through captions that the product information does not support.'),
('A conversation before a collection','BeeGoods is exploring natural-goods connections around its bee-inspired starting point. A maker introduction is an enquiry, not an acceptance or an order. Tell us what you make, the information available for each product and the practical supply details. We can then discuss whether it fits the developing collection without presenting an unconfirmed partnership as a finished collaboration.')]),
('small-gift-big-thought','A small gift can carry a good story','Gifting','collection-concept','Choose a reason for the gift before choosing more things to put in the box.',[
('Give the gift a simple purpose','A thank-you, a welcome and a celebration are different moments. Name the moment first. That gives you a way to choose an object that feels considered, rather than filling a basket because there is room left. A short personal note often explains the choice better than a long list of brand adjectives.'),
('Keep the combination coherent','If you combine products, give each one a role. Something for the table, an object for the home or a useful textile can form a quiet theme without every item matching. Respect the recipient’s food, fragrance and material preferences. Keep the original labels and product information available rather than removing them to make the wrapping look cleaner.'),
('Plan with real constraints','For a group gift or an event, work backwards from the required date, quantity and budget. Ask what is genuinely available and which details can be confirmed in writing. BeeGoods is developing gift ideas and welcomes that kind of practical enquiry. The concepts shown here are starting points for a conversation, not a promise of stock, delivery dates or a particular finished hamper.')]),
('linen-and-everyday-rituals','Useful textiles, quieter everyday moments','Everyday goods','collection-concept','Start with what an object needs to do, then decide whether its material and design suit the task.',[
('Begin with a real use','A cloth for a table, a bag for carrying a few purchases and a wrap for a gift have different jobs. Describe the job before choosing a fabric or colour. That makes it easier to ask useful questions about size, construction and care. A natural-looking texture alone does not tell you how an item will perform in your routine.'),
('Read the material description','Look for the actual fibre description and maker’s care guidance. Do not infer a material from a beige colour or a rustic photograph. If a blend is used, the blend matters. If the business describes a certification or a sourcing commitment, ask how that applies to the particular product rather than treating it as a promise about every item in the range.'),
('Room for thoughtful additions','Textiles are an adjacent collection idea for BeeGoods, not a confirmed line. We are interested in objects that feel useful as well as inviting. A maker can help by explaining the intended use, construction, dimensions and care requirements in plain language. That gives a potential collection more substance than a mood board and helps people understand what they would actually receive.')]),
('a-good-maker-story','A good maker story starts with specifics','Maker stories','craft','Show the work, name the material and make the next step clear.',[
('Make the introduction concrete','Tell someone what you make before telling them that you are passionate about making it. Name one product or a focused range, the materials involved and the kind of work you do yourself. If other businesses contribute components or production, explain their role accurately. A clear introduction gives the visitor a foundation for the rest of the story.'),
('Use images as evidence carefully','A studio photograph can show a working process; a styled product image can explain a finished object. Label them accordingly. Concept artwork belongs in a different category from a photograph of stock ready to supply. Give collaborators appropriate credit and use images you have permission to share. A caption can make those distinctions without interrupting the visual experience.'),
('Connect story and practical details','The next step should be as clear as the story. Include a way to ask about the item and enough information for a useful first message. BeeGoods maker enquiries should identify the proposed products, current stage and available supply information. We are building relationships carefully, so a story on its own does not imply a listing, purchasing commitment or confirmed partnership.')]),
('market-stall-to-web-page','From a market stall to a useful product page','Brand building','collection-concept','Bring the clarity of a good in-person introduction onto the screen.',[
('Remember the questions people ask','At a stall, a visitor can pick something up and ask what it is, who made it and how it is used. A product page needs to carry some of that conversation. Start with the questions your visitors actually ask instead of filling the page with general claims about quality. Specific answers make the object easier to understand.'),
('Choose the views that explain it','Use an overall view, an appropriate detail and an image that makes scale understandable. Keep the product and its variants clear. If props appear in a scene, explain what is included in the offer. Good images reduce confusion when they are paired with accurate descriptions; they do not replace the need to confirm material, quantity and availability.'),
('Make the route onward straightforward','A developing brand may need an enquiry route before it needs a cart. A ready-to-sell product needs confirmed transaction details and a working purchase flow. BeeGoods is currently at the enquiry-led stage. We are building the visual story and collection structure first, while keeping the public next step honest about what a visitor can do today.')]),
('bee-goods-and-med-bee-tate','BeeGoods and Med Bee Tate: connected, with different jobs','Our story','medbeetate-space','A place-based concept and a goods brand can share a thread without becoming the same website.',[
('The place and the everyday','Med Bee Tate is developing a bee-inspired greenhouse, meditation and rest-space concept in Langley. BeeGoods gives the related goods direction its own home. The connection is a shared starting point and creative mood, not a claim that one product reproduces an experience or delivers a health outcome.'),
('A distinct reason to visit each site','Someone exploring a proposed space wants to see the setting and understand its stage of development. Someone exploring goods wants to understand the objects, makers and product details. Those are different conversations. Separate sites let each brand answer its own questions while linking to the other when the connection is useful.'),
('Follow the current stage','Visit Med Bee Tate for its concept and opening updates. Use BeeGoods to explore the collection direction and send a relevant enquiry. Neither a linked brand nor a concept picture should be read as an announcement that a venue is open or an item is in stock. The aim is a connected portfolio with clear next steps, rather than a single page trying to promise everything at once.')]),
('what-to-send-a-collection-buyer','Introducing your goods: a maker’s checklist','For makers','craft','A focused first message helps us understand both the work and the practical fit.',[
('Lead with the proposed range','Tell us which specific goods you want to introduce. Include a clear description, the maker’s name and a small selection of images you are permitted to share. A short, well-described range is easier to consider than a large folder with no indication of which items are current. Separate finished products from concepts or prototypes.'),
('Bring the practical information','Include sizes, materials or ingredients where relevant, packaging details and your preferred business contact. Explain which supply details are confirmed and which would need discussion. If you have product information, care instructions or relevant documentation, say what is available rather than making a broad claim that everything is covered.'),
('Keep expectations clear','A BeeGoods introduction opens a conversation. It is not an order, a guaranteed listing or confirmation that your goods are part of a launch collection. Tell us the timescale you are working toward and the kind of collaboration you have in mind. We can then respond to a concrete proposal and avoid both sides making assumptions about quantities, prices or launch dates.')]),
('collection-not-catalogue','A collection should have a point of view','Brand building','collection-concept','Adding more items is easy. Explaining why they belong together is the creative work.',[
('Choose the connecting idea','A collection becomes easier to understand when its objects share a reason to be together. That reason might be a material, a use, a maker’s approach or the moment in which someone will use them. It does not require every item to look identical. It does require more thought than placing unrelated products under the same logo.'),
('Leave room for difference','An edible gift, a candle and a textile need different descriptions and buying information. A shared visual style can connect them without erasing those distinctions. Give each category enough space to explain itself. If an item does not fit the collection’s purpose, a separate project may be a better home for it than a forced connection.'),
('Our starting point','BeeGoods begins with a bee-inspired thread and the everyday moments that can grow around it. The range is being developed, so the website introduces directions rather than pretending that a complete catalogue already exists. We want each future addition to earn its place through useful details, a clear maker story and a meaningful relationship to the brand.')]),
('concept-photo-or-stock','Concept, stock or actual product: the caption matters','Behind the build','bees','A clear image credit helps you understand what a picture can—and cannot—tell you.',[
('Three kinds of visual','A photograph of an actual product, licensed illustrative footage and a generated concept can all have a role on a developing website. They do different jobs. An actual product photograph can show the item being discussed. Stock can introduce a subject. A concept can make a design direction visible before a product or place is finished.'),
('Do not let the picture overpromise','A concept honey jar is not proof of a harvest, source or finished package. A candle-making clip does not establish the ingredients of a BeeGoods product. A greenhouse illustration does not show a completed operating venue. Captions help keep those boundaries clear while allowing the site to remain visual and inviting.'),
('What to look for here','BeeGoods labels its collection concepts and credits its stock footage. Confirmed product information will need to stand on its own when products are offered. If an image leaves you unsure about what is actually available, ask. The purpose of a brand story is to help someone understand an idea, not make them guess which parts have already been built.')]),
('before-a-first-order','What needs to be ready before a first order','Launch notes','collection-concept','A beautiful launch page is only one part of becoming ready to sell.',[
('Confirm the offer','Before a first order, a customer needs to know exactly what is being offered: product description, quantity, price, applicable charges and availability. Images, variants and included items need to match that description. If those details are still being decided, an enquiry is a more honest next step than a purchase button that cannot complete a real transaction.'),
('Confirm the fulfilment conversation','The practical questions include where an order can go, how it will be supplied and what support is available if something needs attention. Those answers should be visible before a customer commits. A developing brand should not invent shipping promises, return conditions or delivery times to make a page look finished.'),
('Where BeeGoods is now','This is an enquiry-led brand launch. The collection direction, visual identity and journal are here to explore while product details are developed. There is no online checkout, and sending an email does not place an order or automatically subscribe you. If you are interested in a future collection, tell us what you are looking for so the next conversation can be useful and specific.')])]
